insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at,
  photo_url, photo_alt, photo_credit, photo_source)
values
  ('when-the-noise-next-door-will-not-stop', 'When the Noise Next Door Will Not Stop', 'Noise you cannot control costs more than sleep. Why it makes you so angry, what to do while it is happening, and the conversation with the neighbor that usually goes better than you expect.', 'It starts at eleven. Or it is the bass through the floor, or the dog, or the renovation that was supposed to take three weeks, or a television loud enough to follow the plot through a wall.

You are lying there listening to it, which is the worst possible activity, and the anger you feel is out of all proportion to the volume. That proportion is the part worth understanding, because it is not really about how loud the sound is.

Why noise you cannot control is so hard

The same sound is tolerable when you chose it and intolerable when you did not. That is not weakness. Control over a sound is most of what determines whether it bothers you.

Noise you cannot predict is worse than noise that is constant. A steady hum fades. A sound that might start again in the next ten minutes keeps your attention on it, because part of you stays on duty waiting.

At night it is worse again, because there is nothing else to put your attention on and the stakes are higher. Every minute you are awake, you are also calculating how bad tomorrow is going to be.

Then there is the other person. A noise with a human being behind it carries a question about whether they care, and that question is what turns irritation into something much hotter.

The listening is making it worse

This is the practical heart of it. You cannot make them stop tonight, and you can change what you do while it happens.

Lying in the dark tracking the sound is the single most costly thing you can do, because it trains your attention onto it. The goal is not to stop hearing it. It is to stop monitoring it.

Give the sound competition rather than silence. Steady noise of your own, a fan, a recording of rain, something tedious and continuous, works better than earplugs alone, because it takes away the gaps where you listen for the next one.

Earplugs do help, and most people give up on them too early. The cheap foam ones take about a week to get used to and are better than most of what is sold as a solution.

Move where you sleep if you can, even temporarily. People resist this because it feels like losing, and a week of sleep is worth more than the principle.

And put a limit on the time you spend thinking about it when it is quiet. The daytime rehearsing is a real cost and it is entirely yours to cut.

Talking to them, which usually goes better than expected

Most people put this off for months, and most of the time the conversation is far less dramatic than the version they imagined.

Go early, before you are furious. The conversation you have at week two is a different conversation from the one at month six, and the second one tends to go badly because the anger is audible.

Lead with information, not accusation. I can hear the television through the bedroom wall after eleven, I do not think you know that, is the version that works. People genuinely do not know what carries.

Ask for something specific and small. After eleven, or on weeknights, or the speakers off the floor. A vague request to be quieter gives nobody anything to do.

Say something ordinary and friendly afterward, whenever you next see them. The relationship is the thing that keeps it fixed, and a neighbor you are on terms with is much easier to ask twice.

And go in person rather than leaving a note. Notes read colder than anyone intends, and they usually produce resentment instead of a change.

If it does not stop

Keep a plain record, with dates and times. It is dull and it is what every landlord, management company and local office will ask for first.

Find out who actually has authority before you spend energy. A landlord, a condo board, a local noise office. Complaining energetically to the wrong party is a common way to burn out.

And decide what you are willing to do, and what you are not. Some of these do not get solved, and the honest question becomes whether you can insulate, rearrange, or eventually move. That is a miserable conclusion and it is better than two more years of waiting for someone else to change.

Looking after the anger itself

Even once it is handled, the anger tends to outlast the noise, and it is worth treating separately.

Notice when you have started hearing it when it is not there. Most people in this situation get a stretch of that, and it eases once the alertness comes down.

Get out of the apartment on purpose, regularly. Being somewhere else resets things in a way that nothing you do at home can.

And be careful with the story. He knows and he does not care is usually unprovable, and it is the sentence that does the most damage to you. Keeping it to the facts of the sound keeps the anger a manageable size.

When it is worth more help

If you are not sleeping for weeks, if the anger is frightening you or spilling onto people who did not cause it, if you have stopped using rooms in your own home, or if dread of the evening has become the shape of your day, that is worth bringing to someone.

A therapist can help with the alertness and the anger, which is the part that stays after the noise is dealt with, and your doctor is the right person to tell if the sleep loss has gone on long enough to be affecting your health.', array['Stress', 'Anger']::text[], 0, '2026-11-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-the-noise-next-door-will-not-stop.jpg', 'An apartment building with fire escapes, seen from the street below', 'Photo by Matt Bango via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/apartment-building-VWKJCVGD8F'),
  ('when-you-have-to-keep-someone-elses-secret', 'When You Have to Keep Someone Else''s Secret', 'Someone trusted you with something and now you are carrying it everywhere. Why it weighs so much, what to clarify with the person who told you, and where keeping it stops being the right thing to do.', 'Someone told you something and asked you not to repeat it. A diagnosis. An affair. Money that has gone. A plan to leave. Something they did years ago.

You said of course. You meant it. And now you are carrying something that does not belong to you, in rooms full of people who do not know, and it is heavier than you expected a piece of information to be.

Why a secret weighs

It is not only the effort of not saying it, though that is real.

You have to edit yourself continuously. In every conversation with the people it concerns, part of your attention goes to steering, and steering is tiring in a way that talking is not.

It changes the relationships around it. You now know something about a third person that they do not know you know, and that quietly alters every ordinary exchange with them.

You carry the consequences without any of the say. If it goes badly, you watched it coming. If it comes out, you were part of how long it stayed hidden.

And you usually cannot talk about the thing that is bothering you most, which removes the main way people get through anything.

The question nobody asks at the time

When someone says can you keep this to yourself, almost everyone says yes before knowing what it is. That is a decent instinct and it is also how people end up holding things they would never have agreed to.

It is allowed to ask first. Before you tell me, is this something I will have to keep from anyone in particular. That one sentence prevents most of the trouble, and it is much easier to say than people think.

And it is allowed to say no afterward, carefully. I want to support you and I do not think I can keep this from my wife. That is not a betrayal. It is honest, and it is far better said now than discovered later.

Working out what you actually agreed to

Secrets are rarely defined, and most of the strain comes from the undefined parts.

Ask what the boundary is, plainly. Who specifically am I not telling. Is there an end to this. Can I say that something is going on without saying what.

Ask what they want from you, which is a separate question. Some people want advice, some want it witnessed, and some want it held and never mentioned again. Guessing wrong makes you both uncomfortable.

And establish whether you can refer back to it. Many people find they are allowed to carry a thing and not allowed to speak about it even with the person who told them, which is the loneliest version.

Looking after yourself while you hold it

There are a few things that genuinely reduce the weight, and none of them require breaking anything.

Separate the facts from the feelings. You cannot share the facts, and you can absolutely say to someone close to you that you are carrying something difficult and would rather not explain. That is not a breach, and it ends the pretending that you are fine.

Talk to someone entirely outside it if you need to say the whole thing out loud. A therapist, a helpline, a counselor at work. This is one of the main reasons those exist, and using one is not an indulgence.

Put the worry somewhere. People holding a secret tend to run it in the background constantly. Deciding to think about it at a particular time, for a set stretch, works better than trying not to.

And notice if you have started managing the situation rather than holding the information. Arranging things so it does not come out, steering conversations, covering gaps. That is a different job, and it is far heavier than the one you agreed to.

Where keeping it stops being the right thing

Not every secret should be kept, and it is worth being clear about the line in advance rather than in a crisis.

If someone is in danger, if a child is involved, or if what you were told is still happening to someone who does not know, confidentiality is not the governing concern, and it is reasonable to tell somebody who can act.

Where that is the case, say so to the person if you safely can. I cannot keep this one, and here is what I am going to do. People sometimes handle that far better than you expect, and saying it is fairer than acting silently.

And if you are not sure, get advice from someone who deals with that kind of situation rather than deciding alone at midnight. Being unsure is not a reason to default to silence.

When it has become too much to hold

If this has you sleeping badly, if you dread seeing certain people, if you feel complicit in something you did not do, or if you have started avoiding the person who told you, that is a sign the weight has outgrown the arrangement.

Talking it through with a therapist is both allowed and confidential, which makes it one of the few places the whole thing can be said out loud. Your doctor can point you to what is available if you do not know where to start.', array['Boundaries', 'Self-Care']::text[], 0, '2026-11-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-have-to-keep-someone-elses-secret.jpg', 'The corner of an old wooden trunk with metal clasps', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5903728/photo-image-public-domain-wooden-vintage'),
  ('when-nobody-believed-you-at-the-time', 'When Nobody Believed You at the Time', 'Being doubted is a second injury with its own weight. Why disbelief does lasting damage, what it does to your own account of what happened, and how to make that account solid again.', 'You told someone what happened. A parent, a teacher, a manager, a police officer, a doctor, a friend. And the response was a version of are you sure, or that does not sound like them, or you are making a lot out of nothing.

Maybe you tried twice. Maybe you never tried again. Either way, something happened to you, and then something else happened on top of it, and the second thing has its own weight.

Why not being believed does its own damage

It is tempting to treat this as a footnote to the original event. It is not. It is a separate injury with its own effects.

It takes away the thing that would have helped. Being believed is most of what makes a bad experience bearable, and being doubted removes the one repair that was available.

It teaches you not to say anything. People who were disbelieved once often stop reporting anything at all, including things that have nothing to do with it, because the cost of being doubted turned out to be higher than the cost of staying quiet.

It attacks your own account. If somebody you trusted said it did not happen that way, part of you starts checking, and that checking can run for decades.

And it is usually silent. There is often nobody who knows about the second injury at all, because the first one was never established.

The doubt you turn on yourself

This is the part people find hardest to say out loud, and it is extremely common.

You go over it looking for the exaggeration. You cannot find one and you keep looking, because somebody once implied there was one.

You notice that your memory has gaps, and you treat the gaps as evidence against yourself. Memory of a frightening event is normally patchy, and that is a feature of how fear and memory work rather than a sign that the memory is false.

You compare it to worse things and conclude you are making a fuss. Measuring your experience against somebody else''s is a habit that was taught to you, usually by whoever did not believe you.

And you find yourself needing somebody to confirm it before you are allowed to feel anything about it, which keeps the whole thing in other people''s hands.

Why people did not believe you

Worth knowing, because the reasons are mostly about them, and most people assume they are about themselves.

It was easier not to. Believing you would have meant acting, and acting would have cost them something. That is bleak, and it accounts for a great deal of it.

It did not fit what they thought they knew. People hold on to their picture of a person or a place, and new information gets rejected rather than absorbed.

They were frightened, or out of their depth, or protecting someone, or following a process that did not allow for what you were saying.

None of that is a judgment on whether you were telling the truth, and that is the point. Disbelief is evidence about the listener.

Making your own account solid again

You are allowed to settle this without anyone''s permission, and there are things that genuinely help.

Write it down, once, as plainly as you can, including the parts you are unsure about. Marking uncertainty as uncertainty is what an honest account looks like, and seeing that on paper undercuts the idea that doubt means invention.

Notice the difference between remembering imperfectly and being wrong. Those are not the same thing, and the people who doubted you probably ran them together.

Tell one person who will receive it well, if there is anyone. One good response does a surprising amount, and many people find it works even decades later.

And give yourself the sentence you did not get. Something happened, it affected me, and I am allowed to treat it as real. Say it rather than think it. It feels strange and it does something.

If you are considering telling someone now

Sometimes people want to report it formally, or tell a family member, years after the fact. That can be worth doing, and it is worth preparing for.

Decide what you want from it before you start, because the answer shapes everything. Acknowledgment, a record, safety for somebody else, and consequences for a person are four different goals with four different routes.

Expect it to be hard regardless of the outcome, and line up support for afterward rather than only for the day itself.

And get advice from an organization that handles that kind of report rather than going in alone. They know what the process does and does not offer.

Talking to someone who will take it seriously

A therapist is trained to receive an account without auditing it, which is precisely what was missing, and this is well-worn ground rather than an unusual thing to bring.

If this has been sitting with you for years, if you find yourself unable to trust what you remember, or if talking about it brings a wave of something you cannot ride out alone, that is a reason to see someone rather than keep managing it privately. Your doctor can point you to services, many of them free. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Trauma', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-11-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-nobody-believed-you-at-the-time.jpg', 'A leaf-covered path through misty woods in fall', 'Photo by Sebastian Unrau via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/path-trail-9XDH29XSFK'),
  ('being-the-only-one-who-notices-the-mess', 'Being the Only One Who Notices the Mess', 'The chores are not the job. Keeping track of them is, and that part rarely gets counted. How to tell a fairness problem from a standards problem, and how to hand over the noticing rather than the task.', 'The recycling is full. The dishwasher finished two hours ago. There is a bag by the door that somebody was going to take out. You appear to be the only person in the house who can see any of it, and the resentment that produces is specific and corrosive.

What makes it worse is that the work is not really the work. Noticing is the work, and nobody counts it.

The part that is invisible

Doing a task takes ten minutes. Running a household is a continuous background process, and that is the piece that is unevenly shared.

Someone has to hold the list. What is running out, what is due, whose shoes no longer fit, what has to be booked before the end of the month. That is a job even when nothing is being done.

Someone has to notice at the right time. Seeing the empty bottle is only useful if it happens before the next trip to the store.

And someone has to decide the standard. How clean is clean, how often, what can wait. Whoever holds the standard ends up holding the task.

The other person is usually not lying when they say they would have done it. They would have, eventually, at a threshold that sits well above yours, which is a genuinely different experience of the same room.

Two problems that look like one

Almost every version of this argument stalls because two separate things are being discussed at once.

The first is fairness. Who does how much, and who carries the management rather than the chores. That is negotiable and it is the one worth pressing on.

The second is standards. Yours and theirs are different, and neither is objectively correct. A house run to your threshold is more work than a house run to theirs, and some of what you are doing is a preference rather than a requirement.

Sorting them out is useful because it tells you which part to ask about and which part to loosen. Arguing about fairness while actually meaning standards is how these conversations go in circles for years.

Handing over the noticing, not the task

This is the move that changes things, and most people have never tried it, because it feels like more work in the short term.

Give away whole areas rather than instructions. The laundry is yours, start to finish, including when it happens and when the detergent runs out. Not I will tell you when to do a load.

Accept that it will be done differently, and on a different schedule, and resist redoing it. Redoing it teaches the other person that the job is not actually theirs, and once that is learned it is very hard to unlearn.

Expect a gap. Handed over properly, most things get worse for two or three weeks before they settle. That stretch is the price, and stepping in during it is what sends the whole arrangement back.

And write it down somewhere both of you can see. Spoken arrangements drift back to whoever notices first, which is you.

Asking, in a way that can land

The usual opening is a list of grievances delivered at the end of a bad day, which is understandable and gets nowhere.

Pick a neutral time and name the pattern, not the incident. The bag is not the subject. I am the one keeping track of everything in this house is the subject.

Describe the management rather than the chores. Most people genuinely do not see it, and hearing it named for the first time lands differently from being told they are lazy.

Make a specific proposal. Two areas, entirely yours, you decide when. That is answerable. Do more is not.

And ask what they think is unequal. Sometimes there is a real answer you have not counted, and asking makes the whole thing a negotiation rather than a verdict.

Where your own standards come in

This is the less comfortable half, and it matters.

Some of what you are carrying is genuinely optional. Working out which parts would survive you simply stopping is worth doing honestly, and most people find there is more of it than they expected.

Notice if the mess is doing something else for you. For a lot of people a tidy house is how anxiety gets managed, and no redistribution of chores will touch that part.

And consider what you are showing anyone who is watching, especially children, who learn who notices from what they see rather than from what anyone tells them.

When the resentment is the real problem

If this has become the main feeling you have about someone you live with, if you are keeping a tally, if you are exhausted in a way that sleep does not touch, or if raising it has become impossible because of how it goes, the chores are no longer the issue.

That is good ground for a therapist, on your own or together, and the work is practical: what is actually being asked, what is being heard, and what each of you believes the other should simply know. Your doctor can tell you what is available if you are not sure where to start.', array['Assertiveness', 'Perfectionism']::text[], 0, '2026-11-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/being-the-only-one-who-notices-the-mess.jpg', 'A tiled kitchen counter and sink under a frosted window', 'Photo by The Pic Pac via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/kitchen-sink-L07UXRLREE'),
  ('when-the-weather-keeps-you-indoors', 'When the Weather Keeps You Indoors', 'Two wet weeks can take apart something that was holding steady. Why the gap costs more than the days it takes, how to keep the slot when you cannot keep the activity, and how to restart small.', 'It has been raining for nine days, or it is too cold to be out for more than ten minutes, or it is too hot to move before nine at night. Whatever the walk or the run or the ride was doing for you, it stopped about two weeks ago, and you can feel the difference.

The frustrating part is that you did not decide to stop. The weather decided, and now a thing that was holding steady has quietly come apart.

Why a weather gap costs more than the days it takes

A week off does nothing. What does the damage is what happens around the week.

The habit loses its slot. Most movement runs on a time and a trigger rather than on willpower, and two weeks of not doing it at six o''clock removes the trigger.

The restarting gets harder the longer it waits, and not because of fitness. The first session after a gap carries a weight that an ordinary session does not.

And the story shifts. After three weeks it stops being the weather and starts being a sentence about yourself, which is a much harder thing to argue with.

Keeping the slot rather than the activity

This is the single most useful idea here. Protect the time, not the thing.

Put something in the same slot, however small. Ten minutes of anything at the hour you would have gone out keeps the trigger alive, and the trigger is the part that is expensive to rebuild.

Lower the bar deliberately rather than by accident. Deciding in advance that bad weather means ten minutes indoors is different from failing to do forty-five, even when the result looks identical.

And keep the clothes, the mat, the shoes in the same place. Habits live in objects more than in intentions.

What actually works indoors

Most people''s problem is not that there is nothing to do. It is that the indoor version feels like a poor substitute, so they never start.

Stairs, if you have any. Up and down for ten minutes is more than most people manage on a walk, and it needs no equipment or preparation.

Follow something rather than deciding. A video, a routine, anything with a voice telling you what comes next. Deciding what to do is the step most people fail at, and handing that over removes the whole problem.

Chores count, honestly. Floors, stairs, carrying, cleaning out a closet. It is not a workout and it is movement, and movement is the thing you were missing.

Break it up. Three lots of ten minutes across a day does nearly everything that thirty in one block does, and it fits into a day indoors far better.

And stand up more. In a long stretch of bad weather the real loss is often not the exercise but the hours of ordinary moving around that going outside used to supply.

Getting outside anyway

Worth saying, because the threshold is usually set too high.

Most weather is survivable for fifteen minutes with the right clothes, and the clothes are the actual obstacle. One decent rain jacket changes more than any amount of resolve.

Go shorter rather than not at all. The benefit of being outside does not scale the way people assume, and ten minutes of daylight does a lot, particularly in winter when it is doing something for your sleep as well.

Go earlier in the day in heat and later in cold, and stop treating the usual time as fixed.

And notice the difference between cannot and would rather not. Both are allowed. Confusing them is what produces the guilt.

The part that is not really about weather

Sometimes a gap keeps going after the weather turns, and that is worth paying attention to.

If nothing has restarted a month later, the weather has become the explanation rather than the cause, and the useful question is what else changed around the same time.

If getting out of the house has become difficult in general, or indoors has started to feel safer than it used to, that is different from two wet weeks, and it tends to shrink your life quietly.

And if everything feels heavier, not only the exercise, a long dark stretch can do that to people, and it is worth treating as information rather than as a verdict on your discipline.

Restarting without a production

Make the first one embarrassingly small. Ten minutes, the same day you decide, not Monday. The gap is what makes it hard, so the priority is closing it rather than doing it properly.

Do not try to make up the missed weeks. Nothing is owed, and treating it as a debt is the quickest way to stop again.

And expect the first two to feel worse than they should. That passes within about a week and it is not a measure of anything.

When to talk to someone

If a long indoor stretch has flattened your mood, if your sleep has gone with it, or if you are finding reasons not to leave the house that have outlasted the weather, those are worth mentioning to a doctor or a therapist. Both are ordinary things to bring up, and both respond well to being treated early rather than waited out.', array['Movement', 'Habits']::text[], 0, '2026-11-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-the-weather-keeps-you-indoors.jpg', 'A red umbrella covered in raindrops on a lit city street at night', 'Photo by Gabriel Santiago via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/red-umbrella-45JIYYO371')
